              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "“Today, the history of the umbrella. Umbrellas were first used not for rain but for protection from the sun, in ancient Egypt and China. In China, they were made of paper covered in wax to make them waterproof. In 18th-century England, a man called Jonas Hanway was famous for carrying an umbrella in the rain — people laughed at him, but eventually it became fashionable. The biggest improvement came in 1852, when steel frames replaced heavy wooden ones, making umbrellas much lighter.”"
